WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.780
Gregor Mendel crossed pea plants with purple flowers and pea plants with white

2
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.240
flowers. Every offspring had purple flowers.

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.920
Then those purple plants fertilized themselves.

4
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.180
White flowers came back, in about one plant in four.

5
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.140
Why do you look like your parents, but not exactly?

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.300
And how can we predict the odds?

7
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.973
A gene is a section of DNA.

8
00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:33.533
An allele is one version of a gene.

9
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:39.873
Pea plants have a flower color gene, with a purple allele and a white allele.

10
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:45.753
For most genes, body cells carry two alleles, one from each parent.

11
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:51.853
We write alleles as letters: capital P for purple, small p for white.

12
00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:57.923
A dominant allele shows its trait even when only one copy is present.

13
00:00:58.623 --> 00:01:03.063
A recessive allele shows its trait only when both copies are recessive.

14
00:01:03.723 --> 00:01:05.963
The pair of alleles is the genotype.

15
00:01:06.503 --> 00:01:08.783
The trait you can see is the phenotype.

16
00:01:09.743 --> 00:01:11.823
Same two alleles: homozygous.

17
00:01:12.443 --> 00:01:14.343
Different alleles: heterozygous.

18
00:01:14.343 --> 00:01:21.703
So a Pp plant looks purple, but it still carries a white allele.

19
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:27.167
When a heterozygous plant makes sex cells, its two alleles separate.

20
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:30.087
Each sex cell gets one allele.

21
00:01:31.487 --> 00:01:33.747
Scientists call this the law of segregation.

22
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:40.247
Probability is the chance that something will happen, from zero, never, to one,

23
00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:49.007
always. Each sex cell has a 50 percent chance of carrying P, and a 50 percent chance of carrying small p.

24
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.950
A Punnett square is a grid.

25
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.710
It shows every way one sex cell from each parent can combine.

26
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:02.490
Put one parent's sex cells across the top, and the other parent's down the side.

27
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.470
Cross two heterozygous plants.

28
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:18.270
The boxes read PP, Pp, Pp, and pp. Each box has a 1 in 4 chance.

29
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.370
Three boxes look purple.

30
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:21.990
One looks white.

31
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.073
So where was the white hiding?

32
00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:29.753
Inside each heterozygous plant, as a white allele.

33
00:02:30.793 --> 00:02:36.593
Each offspring had a one in four chance of getting two white alleles, and about one

34
00:02:36.593 --> 00:02:37.773
plant in four did.

35
00:02:38.573 --> 00:02:41.853
But a Punnett square gives chances, not certainties.

36
00:02:42.913 --> 00:02:47.433
Four seeds from two heterozygous plants could include no white plants at all.
